1
00:00:16,757 --> 00:00:26,363
[SPEAKER_01]: This is a verse I would like to read out from the Theragāṭṭha, verse 6.13 by Sīrimandhā.

2
00:00:28,664 --> 00:00:29,425
[SPEAKER_01]: But the section...

3
00:00:29,545 --> 00:00:30,505
[SPEAKER_01]: I won't read the whole thing.

4
00:00:32,867 --> 00:00:36,429
[SPEAKER_01]: The world is beaten down by death and surrounded by old age.

5
00:00:37,650 --> 00:00:42,373
[SPEAKER_01]: A dance of craving has laid it low and it's always fuming with desire.

6
00:00:43,954 --> 00:00:45,875
[SPEAKER_01]: The world is beaten down by death

7
00:00:47,023 --> 00:00:54,569
[SPEAKER_01]: encircled, surrounded by old age, beaten constantly, without respite, like a thief being flogged.

8
00:00:55,829 --> 00:00:58,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Three things are coming, like a wall of flame.

9
00:00:59,132 --> 00:01:02,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Death, disease, and old age.

10
00:01:03,335 --> 00:01:07,618
[SPEAKER_01]: No power can stand before them, and no speed enough to flee.

11
00:01:08,979 --> 00:01:11,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't waste your day, a little or a lot.

12
00:01:13,202 --> 00:01:14,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Every night that passes,

13
00:01:15,178 --> 00:01:16,898
[SPEAKER_01]: It shortens your life by that much.

14
00:01:18,659 --> 00:01:22,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Walking or standing, sitting or lying down, your final night draws near.

15
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:30,882
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no time to be careless.

16
00:01:31,802 --> 00:01:42,445
[SPEAKER_00]: So, walking or standing, sitting or laying down, you should be dwelling on the

17
00:01:44,438 --> 00:01:49,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Ultimate context, which is what death is.

18
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:51,360
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an ultimate context to your life.

19
00:01:52,261 --> 00:01:54,181
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a non-negotiable context.

20
00:01:56,042 --> 00:02:07,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people might think, oh, I must be going through the motions and methods of practicing mindfulness of death, thinking about it when I'm walking, when I'm sitting.

21
00:02:07,986 --> 00:02:12,628
[SPEAKER_00]: That's true only in a sense that you need to discern the most fundamental

22
00:02:15,094 --> 00:02:21,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Always present possibility of death, which is why it makes it such an ultimate context of your life.

23
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:29,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything else within your life can come and go, that has been, could have not been, all these things.

24
00:02:29,805 --> 00:02:40,292
[SPEAKER_00]: But one thing for sure, when life is there, death is its guarantee, as the final outcome of it.

25
00:02:41,693 --> 00:02:43,835
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reason why people would be

26
00:02:44,208 --> 00:02:59,640
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason why people are oppressed and oppressed by their desires, by their intentions, unskillful actions, is because you turn a blind eye to this very factual liability to death.

27
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:05,064
[SPEAKER_00]: So, death... possibility of death is certain.

28
00:03:05,084 --> 00:03:13,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Death is possible in a way of... what is possible is you might die this way or that way, but possibility of death is certain.

29
00:03:14,312 --> 00:03:16,212
[SPEAKER_00]: As an outcome.

30
00:03:16,252 --> 00:03:25,614
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a person would not be turning a blind eye to it, that's already something that's gonna start shaping your life differently.

31
00:03:26,494 --> 00:03:43,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Because many... pretty much everything unwholesome that a person can engage with, that you can engage with, is on account of ignoring the fact that you will die, ignoring the fact that you will not always be healthy, not always be young, being intoxicated by those things.

32
00:03:45,355 --> 00:03:48,096
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you entertain desire, you want more of it.

33
00:03:48,496 --> 00:03:57,699
[SPEAKER_00]: But all you need to do is uncover that fundamental context of, well, certainty of death.

34
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:08,283
[SPEAKER_00]: And it will already prevent you, automatically prevent you from how far you would be able to proliferate down the rabbit hole of desires and so on.

35
00:04:08,383 --> 00:04:10,764
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what mindfulness of death means.

36
00:04:11,604 --> 00:04:13,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, you can do that on a very loose level.

37
00:04:15,353 --> 00:04:23,740
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can start narrowing it down and you realize that you might not see the end of this day.

38
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:26,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Not necessarily you must have to like become neurotic about it.

39
00:04:26,662 --> 00:04:27,603
[SPEAKER_00]: I will die, I will die.

40
00:04:27,623 --> 00:04:33,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Just, no, recognize that possibility is so immediate that you might not see the end of the day.

41
00:04:33,909 --> 00:04:35,270
[SPEAKER_00]: You might not see the end of the morning.

42
00:04:35,950 --> 00:04:37,752
[SPEAKER_00]: You might not have enough time to finish your meal.

43
00:04:38,733 --> 00:04:41,575
[SPEAKER_00]: You might not have enough time to breathe out once you breathe in.

44
00:04:44,061 --> 00:04:47,463
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how much you're not in control of your life fundamentally.

45
00:04:48,944 --> 00:04:57,529
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean that you should be trying to... like you should be repeating to yourself that you will die every second of your day.

46
00:04:58,049 --> 00:04:59,490
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not contemplation of death.

47
00:05:00,170 --> 00:05:03,872
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just mechanical repetition of the idea of what death is for you.

48
00:05:05,633 --> 00:05:10,316
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to understand the context of death, which is that

49
00:05:10,786 --> 00:05:16,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Ultimate peripheral context to everything you actually do in your life.

50
00:05:16,389 --> 00:05:17,329
[SPEAKER_00]: So, hence the mindfulness.

51
00:05:17,349 --> 00:05:21,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever there is a principle of peripheral, there is a principle of mindfulness, peripheral awareness.

52
00:05:22,812 --> 00:05:30,296
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by being mindful of of the context of death in regard to your day-to-day life, that's already mindfulness, practice of mindfulness.

53
00:05:33,498 --> 00:05:37,540
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a person wants to start practicing, eh...

54
00:05:38,603 --> 00:05:41,185
[SPEAKER_00]: That contemplation of death as meditation, yeah.

55
00:05:41,966 --> 00:05:48,410
[SPEAKER_00]: As with any other meditation, withdraw yourself from distractions, from company, and yeah, think about it.

56
00:05:48,450 --> 00:05:57,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Think, like, if you don't know where to start, just think about the nature of death that is inescapable to you.

57
00:05:57,357 --> 00:06:04,783
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, it's gonna come, and you can, which then, you take that further and start thinking, okay, so, what is

58
00:06:05,964 --> 00:06:13,209
[SPEAKER_00]: What would be the real significance then, of this life, that is fully within that control of death?

59
00:06:13,789 --> 00:06:19,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Because whatever you do in this life, cannot make you step outside of the liability to death.

60
00:06:19,893 --> 00:06:21,554
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot basically free you from death.

61
00:06:27,538 --> 00:06:30,500
[SPEAKER_00]: So then you start, when you realize that that order cannot change,

62
00:06:31,284 --> 00:06:32,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what you do.

63
00:06:32,244 --> 00:06:33,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what you distract yourself with.

64
00:06:34,245 --> 00:06:35,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what you accomplish with this life.

65
00:06:36,585 --> 00:06:43,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Fundamentally, that whole thing, as much as you developed it, as far as it proliferated, it's still equally within the domain of death.

66
00:06:44,086 --> 00:06:45,006
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't escape that.

67
00:06:53,628 --> 00:07:00,729
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the practice then would be, eh... establishing that right order.

68
00:07:01,048 --> 00:07:02,869
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't matter what you do in this life.

69
00:07:02,969 --> 00:07:05,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Fundamentally, you're liable to sickness, aging and death.

70
00:07:05,691 --> 00:07:08,513
[SPEAKER_00]: The context is not something you need to keep in front of you and think about.

71
00:07:08,533 --> 00:07:08,893
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll die.

72
00:07:08,913 --> 00:07:09,353
[SPEAKER_00]: I will die.

73
00:07:09,533 --> 00:07:09,653
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

74
00:07:09,793 --> 00:07:11,495
[SPEAKER_00]: Just keep it at the back of your mind, where it is.

75
00:07:12,515 --> 00:07:15,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, while I'm doing this, this and this, I might die.

76
00:07:17,118 --> 00:07:21,961
[SPEAKER_00]: It's highly likely I'll die as a result of, you know, sitting in my room reading a book.

77
00:07:23,042 --> 00:07:29,806
[SPEAKER_00]: But death is equally present there, because my life depends upon working organs, upon beating heart, breathing lungs.

78
00:07:31,508 --> 00:07:42,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to practice mindfulness of death, you see that your life is undermined by the random operation of organs that you have no control over.

79
00:07:46,735 --> 00:07:47,796
[SPEAKER_01]: What about the example of...

80
00:07:48,256 --> 00:07:51,358
[SPEAKER_01]: I gave earlier with the... You're on call.

81
00:07:51,798 --> 00:07:52,418
[SPEAKER_01]: You have a job.

82
00:07:53,338 --> 00:07:55,540
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be called to work at any point.

83
00:07:55,880 --> 00:07:58,521
[SPEAKER_01]: You will be called, because you've chosen that job.

84
00:07:59,621 --> 00:08:00,622
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever you do,

85
00:08:05,561 --> 00:08:07,421
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean by context, exactly.

86
00:08:07,542 --> 00:08:15,245
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not a context you can be indifferent to, because it's a context that undermines and affects your life.

87
00:08:15,265 --> 00:08:15,625
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah.

88
00:08:15,945 --> 00:08:22,428
[SPEAKER_00]: And, oh, yeah, the simile of that would be, for example, let's say you have, what would it be?

89
00:08:22,448 --> 00:08:23,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, like a job interview.

90
00:08:25,149 --> 00:08:29,191
[SPEAKER_00]: You have one shot at something that can change your life in a week's time.

91
00:08:29,895 --> 00:08:34,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you do for that week, this will be at the back of your mind, because it is so important.

92
00:08:36,016 --> 00:08:36,536
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will be...

93
00:08:36,576 --> 00:08:36,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

94
00:08:36,976 --> 00:08:39,077
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a degree of concern in regard to it.

95
00:08:39,837 --> 00:08:42,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, imagine if that one shot, it's basically death.

96
00:08:43,737 --> 00:08:46,398
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like a random interview that can go either way.

97
00:08:47,678 --> 00:08:51,239
[SPEAKER_00]: It's as fundamental as losing your life.

98
00:08:51,279 --> 00:08:58,460
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize what kind of concern would that be, peripherally, and how much influence would that have on your choices and decisions in this life.

99
00:08:59,629 --> 00:09:00,970
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the dhammapada verse.

100
00:09:01,530 --> 00:09:06,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it was about anger, wasn't it, in the very beginning, where it said... how do you go?

101
00:09:07,855 --> 00:09:14,339
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's those... yeah, those who quarrel, they forget that we are all gonna die, basically.

102
00:09:14,359 --> 00:09:16,140
[SPEAKER_00]: That we are all sentenced to death in this life.

103
00:09:16,861 --> 00:09:20,283
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, quarrels persist and quarrels develop.

104
00:09:21,043 --> 00:09:26,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't forget the ultimate appointment with death that you have,

105
00:09:27,678 --> 00:09:38,845
[SPEAKER_00]: That can... and also, again, you can't have... like, you know, you can have a job appointment exactly... exact time, exact day, but appointment with death is uncertain in the sense that it can arise at any given time.

106
00:09:40,806 --> 00:09:45,629
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize that appointment might be today, might be tomorrow, might be in ten years' time.

107
00:09:46,790 --> 00:09:47,831
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't forget about it.

108
00:09:48,151 --> 00:09:51,073
[SPEAKER_00]: I cannot forget about it because it can be here the next moment.

109
00:09:52,234 --> 00:09:54,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I forget about it, I might fail.

110
00:09:57,412 --> 00:10:06,635
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever you do then in this life, you do it on the basis of not forgetting about the ultimate context of your liability to die.

111
00:10:06,695 --> 00:10:09,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Certain possibility of death.

112
00:10:11,217 --> 00:10:19,540
[SPEAKER_00]: And that already you could see how much less you would be able to be pulled by the fleeting desires of the senses or distractions and so on.

113
00:10:19,560 --> 00:10:24,002
[SPEAKER_00]: You just... you just would not make... it would not have any value.

114
00:10:25,538 --> 00:10:40,049
[SPEAKER_00]: And, again, if you ever... if a person has any doubt about it, just recollect when you feared for your life, for a period of time, on account of anything, illness or accident or whatever, and see how much interest you had in the world during that period of time.

115
00:10:41,030 --> 00:10:41,991
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, very little.

116
00:10:42,931 --> 00:10:44,152
[SPEAKER_00]: You were not enjoying your food.

117
00:10:45,453 --> 00:10:49,036
[SPEAKER_00]: You were not finding solace in friends and distraction and parties or whatever else.

118
00:10:49,176 --> 00:10:51,598
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just... this was the most pressing thing.

119
00:10:53,219 --> 00:10:54,080
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's basically...

120
00:10:54,617 --> 00:10:58,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Reconnecting to that context that is there, so you don't have to fabricate it.

121
00:10:59,038 --> 00:11:07,501
[SPEAKER_00]: You just have to stop covering it up with that constant, like, influx of sensuality and the intentions for distraction.

122
00:11:09,522 --> 00:11:23,666
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you always feel like you're on call, that you might be called upon by death, that's already means you already develop fundamentally that proper context.

123
00:11:24,487 --> 00:11:30,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Which now shapes your decisions, makes you non-careless in regard to things in front of you in your life.

124
00:11:31,389 --> 00:11:35,310
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's essentially what mindfulness of death is.

125
00:11:35,350 --> 00:11:36,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of the certain.

126
00:11:38,051 --> 00:11:50,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, today I have to do this, I have to work in the garden, and I have to go this, I have to go supermarket, or... and you... All these things can stay, but you would see how much less emotionally you would be pulled or affected by

127
00:11:51,277 --> 00:11:56,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Any of those things, whether they change, whether they're the way you wanted them to be or they go the other way, you wouldn't be affected.

128
00:11:56,458 --> 00:11:56,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

129
00:11:56,638 --> 00:11:57,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you were not there with them.

130
00:11:57,959 --> 00:11:58,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

131
00:11:58,179 --> 00:11:59,479
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're concerned about death.

132
00:12:02,580 --> 00:12:05,881
[SPEAKER_00]: But that kind of practice can be a bit too sharp for some people.

133
00:12:07,621 --> 00:12:09,122
[SPEAKER_00]: That concern can overwhelm you.

134
00:12:10,262 --> 00:12:14,803
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it doesn't, you could see how quickly you would develop this passion in regard to everything that comes your way.

135
00:12:16,304 --> 00:12:17,284
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the Buddha praised it.

136
00:12:18,504 --> 00:12:27,369
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much the quickest way to free your mind from any unwholesome state and ignorance and avoidance of responsibility and so on.

137
00:12:28,310 --> 00:12:33,753
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it's too much, then you practice mindfulness of breathing, because it results in the same, as we spoke in the other talks.

138
00:12:35,234 --> 00:12:46,501
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize that your fully controlled act of breathing depends on things that are not your own, while you're controlling it, which means your own sense of control is undermined by fundamentally depending upon things you cannot control.

139
00:12:49,670 --> 00:13:04,358
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that verse you mentioned, you know, dhammapada, it's also in the Therakārtha, others don't understand that our lives must have limits, or our lives have limits.

140
00:13:04,738 --> 00:13:07,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Those who do understand this, settle their quarrels right away.

141
00:13:08,401 --> 00:13:08,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

142
00:13:09,541 --> 00:13:09,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

143
00:13:10,562 --> 00:13:16,345
[SPEAKER_01]: You're saying it's the most important thing to your life, is the fact that you have an appointment

144
00:13:16,608 --> 00:13:20,411
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you... like, you can also use that in the reverse order and you can see.

145
00:13:20,932 --> 00:13:23,234
[SPEAKER_00]: If you think, oh, but I am practicing mindfulness of death.

146
00:13:23,314 --> 00:13:24,114
[SPEAKER_00]: I am making the effort.

147
00:13:24,475 --> 00:13:24,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

148
00:13:25,035 --> 00:13:26,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Is my mind getting angry?

149
00:13:26,416 --> 00:13:27,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Is my mind getting lustful?

150
00:13:27,617 --> 00:13:30,940
[SPEAKER_00]: If the answer is yes, it means you have an established context of death.

151
00:13:31,741 --> 00:13:35,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you were, you would not... these things would not be able to arise, let alone persist.

152
00:13:37,606 --> 00:13:42,110
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be doing trivial things or getting wound up, triggered by trivial matters.

153
00:13:42,190 --> 00:13:42,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

154
00:13:42,830 --> 00:13:43,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

155
00:13:45,670 --> 00:13:46,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Inconceivable.

156
00:13:46,370 --> 00:13:56,414
[SPEAKER_00]: The only time, the only way you can be triggered by trivial matters, or non-trivial matters, the only way you could be emotionally triggered by anything, is if you have lost the context of certain death.

157
00:13:56,855 --> 00:13:58,495
[SPEAKER_00]: That's you and yours only.

158
00:14:01,556 --> 00:14:05,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, literally, nothing else matters, because everything is within that context.

159
00:14:07,199 --> 00:14:07,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything.

160
00:14:08,219 --> 00:14:15,362
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing you can do, say or think, that will not be within the context of life that's undermined by death.

161
00:14:16,005 --> 00:14:16,585
[SPEAKER_00]: You just cannot...

162
00:14:18,147 --> 00:14:23,250
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the practice of mindfulness of death, as I said, it's not a mechanical repetition of certain thoughts.

163
00:14:23,310 --> 00:14:26,633
[SPEAKER_00]: I will die or trying to keep it in front of you and not forget about it.

164
00:14:27,233 --> 00:14:33,257
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really just clarifying the understanding of that inherent context in whatever you do.

165
00:14:34,678 --> 00:14:41,823
[SPEAKER_00]: The context of that ultimate final cessation that that you cannot even conceive escaping from.

166
00:14:41,863 --> 00:14:45,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when you think about escaping from, the thoughts of that escape are within.

167
00:14:47,595 --> 00:14:48,535
[SPEAKER_00]: The context of death.

168
00:14:48,756 --> 00:14:50,036
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're fully enclosed by it.

169
00:14:50,997 --> 00:14:52,498
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it can be quite frightening.

170
00:14:52,678 --> 00:14:54,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Like being buried alive, for example.

171
00:14:55,499 --> 00:14:59,021
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still alive, but you realize the escape from this is inconceivable.

172
00:14:59,681 --> 00:15:01,602
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the simile that Buddha gave to that king.

173
00:15:02,222 --> 00:15:06,785
[SPEAKER_00]: When he said, what if I tell you there is a big mountain coming to crush you from... An army.

174
00:15:07,005 --> 00:15:08,726
[SPEAKER_00]: An army coming from the east.

175
00:15:09,306 --> 00:15:09,946
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll go west.

176
00:15:10,006 --> 00:15:11,067
[SPEAKER_00]: What if it's coming from the west?

177
00:15:11,167 --> 00:15:11,787
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll go north.

178
00:15:11,827 --> 00:15:13,288
[SPEAKER_00]: What if it's coming from west, north and east?

179
00:15:13,348 --> 00:15:13,828
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll go south.

180
00:15:13,848 --> 00:15:15,229
[SPEAKER_01]: What if it's coming from all four directions?

181
00:15:17,692 --> 00:15:21,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Mountains enclosing you and you can't escape, you can't fight against it.

182
00:15:22,213 --> 00:15:23,013
[SPEAKER_00]: What would you do then?

183
00:15:23,573 --> 00:15:28,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, well, then I will re-evaluate my life and maybe become a monk and try and free myself internally.

184
00:15:29,675 --> 00:15:31,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, that's what life is.

185
00:15:31,735 --> 00:15:35,856
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no escape in any direction from that liability to death.

186
00:15:38,857 --> 00:15:45,018
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's on the basis of simply ignoring that obvious truth, not letting it sink in,

187
00:15:46,249 --> 00:15:50,691
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people get up to all sorts of unwholesome, horrible things as well.

188
00:15:51,672 --> 00:15:53,893
[SPEAKER_00]: People can do just on account of not...

189
00:15:53,993 --> 00:15:55,474
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the duty, isn't it?

190
00:15:56,755 --> 00:15:57,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, pretty much.

191
00:15:57,555 --> 00:16:00,977
[SPEAKER_00]: If you do that, you don't have to worry about most of the other things that you worry about.

192
00:16:00,997 --> 00:16:02,198
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what you've got to do.

193
00:16:02,218 --> 00:16:02,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

194
00:16:02,758 --> 00:16:03,459
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you replace.

195
00:16:03,679 --> 00:16:04,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is better?

196
00:16:04,339 --> 00:16:14,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Thousands of worries that overwhelm you every single day or just develop one fundamental concern, whereby everything else is included and nothing can affect you on account of it.

197
00:16:14,205 --> 00:16:14,985
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a good deal.

198
00:16:16,104 --> 00:16:17,865
[SPEAKER_00]: But, not for the mind that's not trained.

199
00:16:18,025 --> 00:16:19,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Not for the mind that hasn't developed virtue.

200
00:16:19,926 --> 00:16:21,286
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't see that as a good deal.

201
00:16:21,347 --> 00:16:23,327
[SPEAKER_00]: They just see that as a direct threat and terror.

202
00:16:23,788 --> 00:16:23,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

203
00:16:24,048 --> 00:16:34,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they still have a view that sensuality, distraction, acts of will-will, anything, any little things that you do based on passion, they still value that as a mean of escape.

204
00:16:35,393 --> 00:16:40,376
[SPEAKER_00]: They imply that if I don't think about death, I'm not affected by it.

205
00:16:40,956 --> 00:16:43,297
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not like a rational thought that they pre-planned.

206
00:16:44,201 --> 00:16:44,381
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

207
00:16:44,401 --> 00:16:47,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Simply, the habit of ignoring maintains that ignorance.

208
00:16:49,262 --> 00:16:52,903
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you feel like you're sheltered from death.

209
00:16:53,583 --> 00:16:56,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Until, of course, circumstances remind you that it's otherwise.

210
00:16:57,644 --> 00:17:01,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Until you have little pangs of anxiety that then you can't get rid of for the rest of your life.

211
00:17:01,224 --> 00:17:01,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

212
00:17:01,384 --> 00:17:04,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you never admit to yourself what's obvious right from the start.

213
00:17:06,926 --> 00:17:10,466
[SPEAKER_00]: That as soon as you're born, you're old enough to die, pretty much.

214
00:17:11,787 --> 00:17:13,947
[SPEAKER_00]: The second you come... you're conceived,

215
00:17:14,944 --> 00:17:15,844
[SPEAKER_00]: You're liable to death.

216
00:17:17,025 --> 00:17:19,586
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

217
00:17:21,146 --> 00:17:23,907
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's really not even a negotiable context.

218
00:17:25,147 --> 00:17:28,809
[SPEAKER_00]: But people might say, oh, you shouldn't be thinking about this because you won't enjoy life.

219
00:17:29,189 --> 00:17:31,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you won't enjoy life in an unwholesome sense.

220
00:17:32,150 --> 00:17:34,290
[SPEAKER_00]: But you certainly appreciate it in a wholesome sense.

221
00:17:35,691 --> 00:17:39,372
[SPEAKER_00]: As a possibility of developing wisdom, they will then free you from death.

222
00:17:39,392 --> 00:17:42,533
[SPEAKER_00]: As a possibility for doing good and maybe showing others who want to hear.

223
00:17:43,533 --> 00:17:51,557
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is still plenty you can do in this life within the context of death that won't be as futile as simply distracting yourself from the obvious truths.

224
00:17:53,737 --> 00:18:03,162
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to, as you're saying, open up to the fact of death.

225
00:18:03,242 --> 00:18:04,142
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to open up.

226
00:18:04,722 --> 00:18:08,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't close yourself off to that idea, to that thing.

227
00:18:08,684 --> 00:18:10,745
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you close yourself, it will be out of fear.

228
00:18:11,613 --> 00:18:21,319
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, okay, you might not be able to take it all in at once, which is fine, but you should certainly not develop a view that you shouldn't be taking it at all, because it's making you scared.

229
00:18:21,920 --> 00:18:22,080
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

230
00:18:22,180 --> 00:18:28,924
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be developing then yourself in regard to that which scares you, so whereby you won't run away from that which frightens you.

231
00:18:32,026 --> 00:18:33,307
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you get to understand it.

232
00:18:42,271 --> 00:18:57,336
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha says that monks should think, um, I could die today, a snake could bite me, I could fall down, the elements might change and destroy me, my body might get sick, my heart, centipedes, whatever it is.

233
00:18:57,656 --> 00:18:58,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Accidents.

234
00:18:58,697 --> 00:19:01,658
[SPEAKER_00]: And if he thinks, oh, this could happen, that could happen, this could happen.

235
00:19:01,798 --> 00:19:01,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

236
00:19:02,558 --> 00:19:08,980
[SPEAKER_00]: So he doesn't go mindlessly through the motions of snake might bite me, I might fall, my heart might stop.

237
00:19:09,673 --> 00:19:09,793
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

238
00:19:10,173 --> 00:19:17,697
[SPEAKER_00]: He thinks these things to re-evoke the context of I am susceptible to dying at any given moment.

239
00:19:18,337 --> 00:19:21,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Having established that context, he doesn't need to keep thinking about it.

240
00:19:21,899 --> 00:19:29,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Because now, whatever he does already maintains the context, because that already is implying, yeah, it can end at any given moment, because you can die.

241
00:19:29,543 --> 00:19:31,064
[SPEAKER_01]: There's poisonous snakes in the field.

242
00:19:32,104 --> 00:19:32,724
[SPEAKER_01]: It could kill you.

243
00:19:33,265 --> 00:19:33,705
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't...

244
00:19:34,324 --> 00:19:35,945
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to repeat yourself.

245
00:19:36,206 --> 00:19:37,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

246
00:19:37,707 --> 00:19:38,607
[SPEAKER_00]: The snakes are in the field.

247
00:19:38,688 --> 00:19:40,709
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

248
00:19:40,789 --> 00:19:43,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness, properly done, it's on the level of knowledge.

249
00:19:43,892 --> 00:19:45,873
[SPEAKER_00]: You know means the thing is there, present.

250
00:19:46,013 --> 00:19:47,214
[SPEAKER_00]: The context is there, present.

251
00:19:47,234 --> 00:19:47,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you know it.

252
00:19:49,576 --> 00:19:53,499
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why mindfulness and memory recollecting, it goes hand in hand.

253
00:19:54,140 --> 00:20:00,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not a mechanical focusing technique that you will be doing on account of different meditation objects.

254
00:20:00,745 --> 00:20:03,027
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's really developing the knowledge of the context.

255
00:20:03,447 --> 00:20:04,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there are snakes in the field.

256
00:20:07,050 --> 00:20:09,472
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't go around, snakes are in the field, snakes are in the field.

257
00:20:09,992 --> 00:20:12,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever you step in the field, that's the context of the field.

258
00:20:13,836 --> 00:20:15,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't actively think about it.

259
00:20:16,218 --> 00:20:24,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever a little grass moves and touches you, and you know that from experience, moves and touches your ankle, you are there, instantaneously.

260
00:20:24,124 --> 00:20:31,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the context is there, because it's fully understood and became part of being in the field, walking in the wilderness.

261
00:20:31,710 --> 00:20:33,750
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't think about it, because it's there now.

262
00:20:34,250 --> 00:20:37,651
[SPEAKER_00]: It's became part of the significance of the thing that you're experiencing.

263
00:20:38,611 --> 00:20:40,572
[SPEAKER_00]: But that cannot be done mindlessly.

264
00:20:40,592 --> 00:20:44,432
[SPEAKER_00]: It becomes part of the significance when you understood it as part of significance.

265
00:20:45,752 --> 00:20:46,453
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you want.

266
00:20:46,713 --> 00:20:48,093
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we mean watchfulness.

267
00:20:48,953 --> 00:20:50,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Like you don't forget about it.

268
00:20:50,513 --> 00:20:54,154
[SPEAKER_00]: I think even Ajahn Chah gave a similar example.

269
00:20:54,174 --> 00:20:58,535
[SPEAKER_00]: It was like a mother that has to go and do the work outside and leave the baby inside.

270
00:21:00,055 --> 00:21:00,255
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

271
00:21:01,028 --> 00:21:04,650
[SPEAKER_00]: She's out there only for the amount of time, minimum amount she has to finish the work.

272
00:21:05,030 --> 00:21:05,850
[SPEAKER_00]: She doesn't linger.

273
00:21:06,210 --> 00:21:08,772
[SPEAKER_00]: She doesn't forget about her baby.

274
00:21:09,572 --> 00:21:11,253
[SPEAKER_00]: She doesn't distract herself from it.

275
00:21:12,173 --> 00:21:17,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Means whatever she's doing, the back of her mind still maintains the importance of a certain context.

276
00:21:18,636 --> 00:21:20,577
[SPEAKER_00]: And when she's done, she goes back to it.

277
00:21:20,817 --> 00:21:25,700
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's like the development of mind, the development of mindfulness, the importance of their watchfulness.

278
00:21:26,600 --> 00:21:30,442
[SPEAKER_01]: If she doesn't go down the street, leave the kid alone, or get drunk, maybe something.

279
00:21:31,062 --> 00:21:32,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, I suppose.

280
00:21:33,003 --> 00:21:36,265
[SPEAKER_00]: If she cares, if she has concern about the child, she wouldn't be doing it.

281
00:21:36,646 --> 00:21:38,467
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a boundary for her.

282
00:21:38,567 --> 00:21:41,449
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why would I then be breaking the precepts?

283
00:21:41,509 --> 00:21:42,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would I then be careless?

284
00:21:42,909 --> 00:21:46,472
[SPEAKER_00]: When I might die within 10 seconds, my heart might stop.

285
00:21:46,812 --> 00:21:47,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it probably won't.

286
00:21:47,833 --> 00:21:49,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but it might.

287
00:21:49,514 --> 00:21:53,496
[SPEAKER_00]: That's more than enough for that right concern, right type of concern.

288
00:21:54,917 --> 00:21:59,680
[SPEAKER_00]: See, like, you know, like the Noble Eightfold Path, the right view, right intention and so on.

289
00:22:00,219 --> 00:22:14,506
[SPEAKER_00]: You could say that the entire Noble Eightfold Path is predicated on developing right concern, being concerned rightly, becoming aware of that existential anxiety or dread, whatever you want to call it.

290
00:22:14,987 --> 00:22:22,370
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could say it's a right concern that comes through right responsibility, and then the right view will come out of it.

291
00:22:26,332 --> 00:22:27,333
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the Buddha meant as well.

292
00:22:27,353 --> 00:22:29,454
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, I'm teaching suffering and freedom from suffering.

293
00:22:30,091 --> 00:22:33,732
[SPEAKER_00]: First means you need to know how to start suffering properly.

294
00:22:33,772 --> 00:22:36,313
[SPEAKER_00]: The right concern, the right displeasure, yeah.

295
00:22:37,053 --> 00:22:39,274
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature of, oh, hold on, this is not a choice.

296
00:22:39,894 --> 00:22:41,534
[SPEAKER_00]: This is not negotiable either.

297
00:22:41,594 --> 00:22:42,654
[SPEAKER_00]: This concerns you.

298
00:22:42,895 --> 00:22:43,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

299
00:22:43,875 --> 00:22:45,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Only me.

300
00:22:45,075 --> 00:22:47,416
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be compared to other concerns, to other people.

301
00:22:47,456 --> 00:22:47,576
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

302
00:22:47,656 --> 00:22:49,016
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just my and my only.

303
00:22:49,476 --> 00:22:51,177
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is the only thing worth dealing with.

304
00:22:51,237 --> 00:22:53,077
[SPEAKER_00]: The only thing you can deal with, truly.

305
00:22:54,958 --> 00:22:58,999
[SPEAKER_00]: So, developing the right concern, develops the right Noble Eightfold Path.

306
00:23:02,775 --> 00:23:13,403
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mindfulness of death or the practice of death, again, just sit quietly and think about... well, you could even think about your beating heart or your breathing lungs.

307
00:23:15,465 --> 00:23:16,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, don't negate nothing.

308
00:23:16,846 --> 00:23:17,607
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing to negate.

309
00:23:17,627 --> 00:23:21,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Just be aware that, like, I am breathing now, but I might not be able to.

310
00:23:22,371 --> 00:23:22,711
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

311
00:23:22,731 --> 00:23:26,674
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the practice of trying to think about the different ways that you could die.

312
00:23:27,319 --> 00:23:28,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

313
00:23:28,300 --> 00:23:29,880
[SPEAKER_00]: But that only can be indirect.

314
00:23:30,040 --> 00:23:30,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, see, that's the thing.

315
00:23:31,101 --> 00:23:36,664
[SPEAKER_00]: People only regard things that they can think about actually directly in front of me, as something that I developed.

316
00:23:36,744 --> 00:23:36,884
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

317
00:23:37,284 --> 00:23:40,225
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to be discerning the context of what's in front of you.

318
00:23:41,106 --> 00:23:47,629
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you keep making that context as that in front of you, means the context of that is not in front of you.

319
00:23:48,530 --> 00:23:51,231
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, there is something peripheral to whatever it's in front of you.

320
00:23:51,831 --> 00:23:53,052
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you want to discern.

321
00:23:53,332 --> 00:23:55,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Like what we spoke in the talk Peripheral Awareness.

322
00:23:56,066 --> 00:24:01,209
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you're thinking about death, you can't be just visualizing how you're dying, because that's not what death is for you at the time.

323
00:24:02,029 --> 00:24:11,754
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a particular exemplification of that possibility, but you're not understanding the certainty of it, because certainty of death, of the possibility of death, is on the level of the context.

324
00:24:15,096 --> 00:24:21,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you think... Well, you could, but would it be good to think whatever situation

325
00:24:27,700 --> 00:24:29,681
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have to start like that.

326
00:24:29,781 --> 00:24:31,982
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you'd get a sense of concern.

327
00:24:32,062 --> 00:24:35,563
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you recognize that it's that concern that needs to be developed.

328
00:24:35,643 --> 00:24:39,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Must not be distracted from, must not be covered up, must not be ignored.

329
00:24:41,065 --> 00:24:42,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Must not be trying to get rid of it either.

330
00:24:44,107 --> 00:24:45,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, there's anxiety and concern.

331
00:24:45,447 --> 00:24:46,187
[SPEAKER_00]: I must get rid of it.

332
00:24:46,207 --> 00:24:49,669
[SPEAKER_01]: I must get rid of it.

333
00:24:51,049 --> 00:24:55,131
[SPEAKER_00]: Establishing the concern, the context of death, and then allowing it to endure.

334
00:24:56,546 --> 00:25:07,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, through allowing it to endure, you don't need to then worry about whatever is in front of you, because it's going to be kept in check by the fundamental concern of liability to death, which is already a concern based on dispassion.

335
00:25:08,092 --> 00:25:17,696
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so just as a contemplation, whatever you're doing, try to see that the possibility of death there.

336
00:25:18,316 --> 00:25:23,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I could, like, I'm sitting now, so how could death come up?

337
00:25:23,119 --> 00:25:25,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I could fall off my chair, hit my head,

338
00:25:26,772 --> 00:25:27,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

339
00:25:28,534 --> 00:25:29,695
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I understand what you're saying.

340
00:25:29,755 --> 00:25:39,946
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point I'm making is that you can do that only initially, before it becomes that mechanical just visualizations of how you might die and you lose the connection with it.

341
00:25:40,847 --> 00:25:43,230
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you can't just be focusing on what you're going to think.

342
00:25:44,111 --> 00:25:47,675
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to focus on that emotional background of being liable to death.

343
00:25:48,507 --> 00:25:52,951
[SPEAKER_00]: In which case, you don't need to think particularly, I will die because my heart will stop while I fall off the chair.

344
00:25:53,352 --> 00:25:57,035
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to think, I will die, I could die, and you already feel it.

345
00:25:57,055 --> 00:25:58,516
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's about picking up the sign.

346
00:25:59,137 --> 00:26:00,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Picking up the sign, exactly.

347
00:26:00,638 --> 00:26:05,062
[SPEAKER_00]: The theme, the context, the peripheral of the actual possibilities of death.

348
00:26:05,343 --> 00:26:06,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is the flavor of this life?

349
00:26:10,661 --> 00:26:13,903
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's going to be that kind of initially unpleasant concern.

350
00:26:14,684 --> 00:26:17,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Afterwards, the displeasure of that concern disappears.

351
00:26:17,466 --> 00:26:20,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, it's inconceivable to be otherwise.

352
00:26:21,329 --> 00:26:27,253
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might have apprehension and so on, but none of them would be fear and dread and pain at the prospect of death.

353
00:26:27,333 --> 00:26:27,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

354
00:26:27,493 --> 00:26:30,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not as intoxicated with life as you were before.

355
00:26:31,436 --> 00:26:34,839
[SPEAKER_00]: And you were not intoxicated because you stopped maintaining the intoxication.

356
00:26:35,899 --> 00:26:38,081
[SPEAKER_00]: And that intoxication is maintained through ignoring.

357
00:26:39,956 --> 00:26:43,098
[SPEAKER_00]: The concern about the life and that's liable to death.

358
00:26:44,259 --> 00:26:49,063
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, if you want to do meditation on death, think about like you're beating heart.

359
00:26:50,144 --> 00:26:54,487
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you could hear it, you could feel it beating, but can you have any intentional say?

360
00:26:55,047 --> 00:26:55,688
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize no.

361
00:26:56,188 --> 00:27:04,074
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if this thing stops, in the same sense like a branch falls, cup gets knocked over, the heart stops.

362
00:27:04,795 --> 00:27:09,138
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize the heart is on the level of these random things in the world that can just change.

363
00:27:10,466 --> 00:27:13,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the elements, because of the wind, because of the other... whatever.

364
00:27:14,469 --> 00:27:17,292
[SPEAKER_00]: But if that does happen, my life is over.

365
00:27:17,752 --> 00:27:24,838
[SPEAKER_00]: So my life entirely, my sense of mastery, my sense of control, depends on something as silly as a cup being knocked over.

366
00:27:25,739 --> 00:27:27,701
[SPEAKER_00]: A valve just... A pump, yeah.

367
00:27:27,841 --> 00:27:28,481
[SPEAKER_00]: An electrical pump.

368
00:27:28,501 --> 00:27:31,203
[SPEAKER_00]: A pump just stop... stop ceasing to pump.

369
00:27:32,384 --> 00:27:33,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Or getting blocked.

370
00:27:33,285 --> 00:27:36,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Just the pump gets blocked for 10 seconds, might be enough to kill me.

371
00:27:37,621 --> 00:27:40,522
[SPEAKER_00]: And you... that is inconceivable to have said that.

372
00:27:41,822 --> 00:27:45,202
[SPEAKER_00]: But, obviously, the first reaction to that is just this overwhelming concern.

373
00:27:45,722 --> 00:27:47,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, people feel fully justified.

374
00:27:47,443 --> 00:27:48,443
[SPEAKER_00]: I must not think about this.

375
00:27:50,263 --> 00:27:53,064
[SPEAKER_00]: But once you get used to it, that concern cannot overwhelm you.

376
00:27:53,224 --> 00:27:56,564
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, literally, you have... all you have to do is just remember it.

377
00:27:56,624 --> 00:28:01,225
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're fully back in the... back in the proper practice of that recollection of death.

378
00:28:02,005 --> 00:28:05,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Not through, like, randomly visualizing possibilities of dying.

379
00:28:05,086 --> 00:28:06,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Just... my body,

380
00:28:07,638 --> 00:28:15,543
[SPEAKER_00]: This is a fundamental sort of basis for my whole life, my all intentions, my past, my future, for everything.

381
00:28:16,244 --> 00:28:25,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, it's the basis that is fully accidental and liable to elements and I cannot even conceive having any remote input into that domain or that environment.

382
00:28:25,890 --> 00:28:32,454
[SPEAKER_00]: And that makes you feel like... Well, that prevents you from appropriating your own life, your own intention, your own sense of control.

383
00:28:32,974 --> 00:28:35,256
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why if you're not overwhelmed by fear and anxiety,

384
00:28:36,872 --> 00:28:44,938
[SPEAKER_00]: And you persevere through it, and you fully maintain this context, this passion is the automatic result, arahantship.

385
00:28:46,039 --> 00:28:48,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Die before you die, as Ajahn Chah said.

386
00:28:49,682 --> 00:28:55,647
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what about observing or contemplating death by looking at others' dying?

387
00:28:55,687 --> 00:28:55,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

388
00:28:56,447 --> 00:28:56,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

389
00:28:56,867 --> 00:28:57,248
[SPEAKER_00]: That can...

390
00:28:58,068 --> 00:29:06,535
[SPEAKER_00]: If you use that to re-evoke concern for your own life and death, as a context, emotional context, that you realize

391
00:29:06,974 --> 00:29:09,996
[SPEAKER_00]: I better deal with this while I can, then it's fine.

392
00:29:10,856 --> 00:29:22,942
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you only use it for some sort of like a, you know, at arm's length, so to speak, not really allow it to come in because it's going to overwhelm you, and just try to, you know, observe and learn through observation, that's not death that you're learning about.

393
00:29:23,782 --> 00:29:26,704
[SPEAKER_00]: You're learning about the external manifestation of someone else's death.

394
00:29:27,604 --> 00:29:35,448
[SPEAKER_00]: But unless you allow it to remind you, to reconnect you with your own certain death, impending death,

395
00:29:36,877 --> 00:29:38,118
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no real benefit to it.

396
00:29:38,978 --> 00:29:48,665
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly how people can be dealing with death on a daily basis and still remain fully inauthentic, full cover-up of their own death.

397
00:29:50,066 --> 00:29:50,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Passionate about life.

398
00:29:51,027 --> 00:29:59,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, like you can work in morgue or whatever and it might hit you initially and then you just cover it up and now it's just cadavers basically.

399
00:29:59,773 --> 00:30:03,375
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't even see that as a representation of the life that you are partaking in.

400
00:30:04,568 --> 00:30:06,129
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

401
00:30:06,149 --> 00:30:09,192
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes that... you must stop keeping it at arm's length.

402
00:30:10,172 --> 00:30:15,076
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you don't know how to deal with it, not keeping it at arm's length, it's going to overwhelm you.

403
00:30:15,416 --> 00:30:18,939
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's not optional for people who don't know how to deal with it, to keep it at arm's length.

404
00:30:18,979 --> 00:30:22,242
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the only mean of self-preservation that they know.

405
00:30:23,042 --> 00:30:30,408
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's going to be very frightening and scary when even that gets taken away, when they're actually dying, as that king became aware when the Buddha explained this to him.

406
00:30:37,630 --> 00:30:42,935
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what... that's why those people who would understand what the Buddha's teaching would freak out and they all... they will kill us.

407
00:30:43,475 --> 00:30:56,947
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said, yeah, for any mind that hasn't been sort of restrained, that still values passion, that still values life, that still values, oh, this is mine and I am, these truths will be like frightening, terrifying, overwhelming.

408
00:30:58,848 --> 00:31:01,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Fall on the ground, beat their breast, pull their hair, full despair.

409
00:31:02,672 --> 00:31:04,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they realize this is inescapable.

410
00:31:06,898 --> 00:31:09,580
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to escape it is to stop appropriating it internally.

411
00:31:10,140 --> 00:31:12,582
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to stop appropriating, it means you have to stop appropriating everything.

412
00:31:15,464 --> 00:31:16,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Your likes and your dislikes.

413
00:31:17,766 --> 00:31:26,412
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to, okay, have to see them with wisdom and fully understand it.

414
00:31:32,076 --> 00:31:34,878
[SPEAKER_01]: In that, in the beginning of that sutta that I read out,

415
00:31:36,679 --> 00:31:41,983
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning of this talk, sir, the part I didn't read was the beginning.

416
00:31:43,264 --> 00:31:47,127
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll read now, because it kind of... it might make a bit more sense.

417
00:31:47,147 --> 00:31:47,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, go for it.

418
00:31:48,809 --> 00:31:51,451
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, the rain saturates things that are covered up.

419
00:31:52,672 --> 00:31:54,613
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't saturate things that are open.

420
00:31:55,934 --> 00:31:59,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, you should open up a covered thing, so the rain will not saturate it.

421
00:32:00,758 --> 00:32:03,040
[SPEAKER_01]: The world is beaten down by death, and so on.

422
00:32:15,434 --> 00:32:17,095
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't get a chance to dry.

423
00:32:17,415 --> 00:32:20,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but open it up, and it won't get saturated.

424
00:32:21,657 --> 00:32:31,743
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will get affected by the rain, as in, you're gonna get affected by death, or even by anything unpleasant, for as long as you're covering things up from yourself, basically.

425
00:32:32,884 --> 00:32:42,469
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean, like, all these truths are there, obvious in a way, but you need to make the effort of undoing the cover-up for them to be discerned.

426
00:32:43,913 --> 00:32:45,814
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like finding the new ways out.

427
00:32:45,874 --> 00:32:48,676
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically just stop covering up what's obvious.

428
00:32:49,977 --> 00:32:51,077
[SPEAKER_00]: The certainty of death.

429
00:32:51,878 --> 00:32:53,199
[SPEAKER_00]: The certainty of non-control.

430
00:32:53,379 --> 00:32:54,560
[SPEAKER_00]: The certainty of change.

431
00:32:56,541 --> 00:32:57,461
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact of craving.

432
00:32:57,942 --> 00:32:58,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

433
00:32:58,282 --> 00:33:02,064
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize these things, it's through you ignoring them, i.e.

434
00:33:02,184 --> 00:33:11,350
[SPEAKER_00]: covering them up, then you get liable and you get saturated by the rain, by the grief, by despair, by sickness, aging and death, by everything else that befalls you.

435
00:33:11,803 --> 00:33:15,004
[SPEAKER_00]: It affects you because it's covered up there and it sinks into it.

436
00:33:15,404 --> 00:33:16,144
[SPEAKER_00]: It stays with it.

437
00:33:17,224 --> 00:33:18,285
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't go through with it.

438
00:33:18,725 --> 00:33:19,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Through it, sorry.

439
00:33:20,525 --> 00:33:21,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the point.

440
00:33:22,326 --> 00:33:29,487
[SPEAKER_00]: All these things remain the same for an arahant, but he's not affected by it, because he stopped covering these things up from him, from himself.

441
00:33:30,607 --> 00:33:32,648
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean, like, when I say self-transparent.

442
00:33:34,028 --> 00:33:35,969
[SPEAKER_00]: If something's covered up, it's not transparent.

443
00:33:36,089 --> 00:33:36,969
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't see through it.

444
00:33:39,148 --> 00:33:44,893
[SPEAKER_00]: So, self-transparency, that we spoke about in previous talks, is developed by uncovering things up for yourself.

445
00:33:47,095 --> 00:33:52,980
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever you want to engage in sensuality or do something out of your will, wait.

446
00:33:53,020 --> 00:33:58,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Remind yourself of the fundamental context of liability to death and you realize, oh, this is not worth the effort.

447
00:34:00,626 --> 00:34:01,226
[SPEAKER_00]: It simply isn't.

448
00:34:03,548 --> 00:34:07,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if I tell you, oh, look, you know, go into this place, get in.

449
00:34:08,378 --> 00:34:13,661
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's going to be the greatest central satisfaction of every sense you can imagine.

450
00:34:14,721 --> 00:34:17,303
[SPEAKER_00]: But, there's also 99% chance you're going to die.

451
00:34:21,265 --> 00:34:23,506
[SPEAKER_00]: No amount of that incitement would make you enter it.

452
00:34:25,687 --> 00:34:27,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're just, oh, it's not worth it.

453
00:34:27,448 --> 00:34:37,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Because now... Or if I tell you, like, go in it, go in this place, enjoy food and whatever else, but at the end of it, within half an hour, you'll be dead.

454
00:34:37,943 --> 00:34:38,583
[SPEAKER_00]: You must die.

455
00:34:39,564 --> 00:34:41,805
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't go in it, you live longer.

456
00:34:43,306 --> 00:34:43,966
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't go in it.

457
00:34:46,067 --> 00:34:56,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Because suddenly, the whole enjoyment of sensual distraction and pleasure is placed in an immediate, direct, like, mirroring of the context that it's within death.

458
00:34:57,513 --> 00:34:58,674
[SPEAKER_00]: If I do this, I'm dead.

459
00:34:59,494 --> 00:35:00,715
[SPEAKER_00]: If I don't do it, I'm not dead.

460
00:35:03,296 --> 00:35:05,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if, say, somebody tells you like, oh, you're terminal.

461
00:35:05,398 --> 00:35:06,278
[SPEAKER_00]: You have three days to live.

462
00:35:06,907 --> 00:35:09,989
[SPEAKER_00]: And tomorrow, like today, you had plans for tomorrow.

463
00:35:10,770 --> 00:35:14,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Hanging out with your friends, some great party, careless get-drink, whatever.

464
00:35:16,314 --> 00:35:22,139
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, later on today, you find out that you have three days to live.

465
00:35:22,159 --> 00:35:26,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you still have interest to go around just carelessly partying with random people?

466
00:35:26,782 --> 00:35:26,943
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

467
00:35:27,503 --> 00:35:31,626
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be too concerned about, is there anything I can do before I die, as in to prevent me from dying?

468
00:35:33,327 --> 00:35:35,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there anything that they can make difference to that?

469
00:35:37,270 --> 00:35:38,571
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize that is always the case.

470
00:35:39,131 --> 00:35:41,072
[SPEAKER_00]: As soon as you're born, that's always the context.

471
00:35:41,973 --> 00:35:43,314
[SPEAKER_00]: The time is too short.

472
00:35:43,654 --> 00:35:50,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can die, you will die at any given period in your life.

473
00:35:51,198 --> 00:35:52,779
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no such thing as too soon.

474
00:35:53,760 --> 00:36:01,404
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why we say time is too short, don't be negligent before it's too late.

475
00:36:01,424 --> 00:36:06,367
[SPEAKER_00]: So the context of death immediately de-evaluates, takes value away from anything central.

476
00:36:07,610 --> 00:36:08,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything based on your will.

477
00:36:10,292 --> 00:36:11,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, there you go.

478
00:36:11,793 --> 00:36:12,893
[SPEAKER_00]: You have access to all of this.

479
00:36:13,654 --> 00:36:15,695
[SPEAKER_00]: But, death is waiting for you at the end of it.

480
00:36:16,175 --> 00:36:17,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm not going to do it then.

481
00:36:17,196 --> 00:36:17,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

482
00:36:17,816 --> 00:36:20,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's exactly the same principle that applies to your life.

483
00:36:20,798 --> 00:36:22,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you do in this life, death waits above you.

484
00:36:24,180 --> 00:36:24,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, okay.

485
00:36:24,760 --> 00:36:27,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you automatically become more aware.

486
00:36:28,703 --> 00:36:30,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Just through discerning that context.

487
00:36:30,984 --> 00:36:31,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

488
00:36:34,306 --> 00:36:37,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you remember the appointment.

489
00:36:37,851 --> 00:36:45,814
[SPEAKER_00]: The impending appointment that can be sort of activated at any time, maybe in 50 years, maybe in 5 minutes.

490
00:36:47,995 --> 00:36:49,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Also, does that make me concerned?

491
00:36:49,455 --> 00:36:49,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

492
00:36:49,815 --> 00:36:50,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

493
00:36:51,856 --> 00:37:04,461
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you be concerned about, like, I don't know, something that has no value to you, like some piece of rock, if it gets destroyed somewhere?

494
00:37:05,721 --> 00:37:07,522
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have no emotional connection with it.

495
00:37:08,165 --> 00:37:09,826
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you couldn't care less if you get destroyed.

496
00:37:11,707 --> 00:37:18,712
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, if there is concern about you being destroyed, you realize, oh, so there is certain emotional connection with this.

497
00:37:18,852 --> 00:37:22,174
[SPEAKER_00]: But, is that emotional connection justified?

498
00:37:22,254 --> 00:37:23,775
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you start uncovering.

499
00:37:25,596 --> 00:37:32,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Your sense of entitlement to appropriation of your own feelings, your own intentions, your own sensual pleasures.

500
00:37:33,201 --> 00:37:35,442
[SPEAKER_00]: You start uncovering it, you realize, no, this is not justified.

501
00:37:36,123 --> 00:37:43,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if these things were truly mine, if I were truly the master controller, they would not be liable to death.

502
00:37:43,465 --> 00:37:45,826
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's, that's the, again, the three divine messengers.

503
00:37:46,026 --> 00:37:51,028
[SPEAKER_00]: They're telling you what's beneath it, what you'd refuse to admit to yourself.

504
00:37:52,228 --> 00:37:53,728
[SPEAKER_00]: They're conveying you that message.

505
00:37:56,689 --> 00:38:01,871
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can only fully grasp it if you start uncovering it, start drying it up.

506
00:38:28,453 --> 00:38:43,797
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to meditate on it, set, like, set aside half an hour or whatever you have, without any distractions, think about your liability to death until you develop that context that slightly, or not so slightly, concerns you, and then just allow it to endure.

507
00:38:44,397 --> 00:38:45,657
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're thinking, what do I do next?

508
00:38:45,817 --> 00:38:46,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

509
00:38:46,698 --> 00:38:52,199
[SPEAKER_00]: All I do is make sure that this context remains transparent for this half an hour that I'm meditating.

510
00:38:52,379 --> 00:38:52,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

511
00:38:53,659 --> 00:38:55,820
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if my thoughts start coming, I let them,

512
00:38:56,488 --> 00:38:58,729
[SPEAKER_00]: While I still keep a quarter of an eye on that context.

513
00:39:00,390 --> 00:39:03,151
[SPEAKER_00]: If I... if I go away and... oh, I forgot.

514
00:39:03,432 --> 00:39:04,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you go back to the context.

515
00:39:04,952 --> 00:39:11,676
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, just so to let your mind start going again, by you not losing the sight of the context.

516
00:39:12,476 --> 00:39:16,038
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the practice of mindfulness that results in samādhi that then feeds mindfulness.

517
00:39:17,779 --> 00:39:22,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're learning how to not forget about the context, even when other things are happening.

518
00:39:23,022 --> 00:39:23,562
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's life.

519
00:39:25,207 --> 00:39:30,028
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if whatever's happening in your day-to-day life, then, you still have that context at the back of your mind.

520
00:39:30,048 --> 00:39:32,649
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the unshakable mindfulness, what the Buddha meant by it.

521
00:39:33,649 --> 00:39:35,189
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the proper maraṇasati.

522
00:39:36,390 --> 00:39:45,912
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether I'm eating, taking food, or breathing in, breathing out, the context remains at the back, and I've not lost connection with it.

523
00:39:46,672 --> 00:39:48,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean I must have it all the time in front of me.

524
00:39:48,773 --> 00:39:48,933
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

525
00:39:49,473 --> 00:39:50,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite the opposite.

526
00:39:51,697 --> 00:39:56,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Means I should be able to allow it to go as far as you want, but I still won't completely lose the sight of it.

527
00:39:58,839 --> 00:40:00,859
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you think, am I... have I lost sight?

528
00:40:01,179 --> 00:40:02,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the greed a version of delusion in my mind?

529
00:40:02,920 --> 00:40:07,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the desire for distraction, carelessness, sensuality, ill-will, anger?

530
00:40:07,501 --> 00:40:07,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

531
00:40:08,021 --> 00:40:08,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, the context.

532
00:40:09,001 --> 00:40:10,861
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't say I'm rooted in the context anymore.

533
00:40:11,882 --> 00:40:14,282
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're rooted in the context, these things cannot arise.

534
00:40:14,582 --> 00:40:17,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Amid sensuality, amid irritable things, they can't arise.

535
00:40:20,513 --> 00:40:24,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they require passion, and passion and context of death are mutually exclusive.

536
00:40:25,054 --> 00:40:30,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Passion requires that ignoring the fact that this is all subject to this certain destruction.

537
00:40:32,896 --> 00:40:35,956
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you can't ignore that anymore, means you can't engage in passion anymore.

538
00:40:36,537 --> 00:40:42,698
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what would have meant those people who are not mindful, as in those people who are not aware of that context, they're as if dead already.

539
00:40:43,638 --> 00:40:45,519
[SPEAKER_00]: They're fully under that weight of death.

540
00:40:46,099 --> 00:40:47,039
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to

541
00:40:47,670 --> 00:40:52,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Not partaking it, not be affected by it, is to fully understand that context of death.

542
00:40:53,774 --> 00:41:02,179
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by being unmindful, you're fully subscribed, fully covered up, fully cocooned in that liability of certain death.

543
00:41:02,779 --> 00:41:03,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you're as if dead.

544
00:41:03,760 --> 00:41:06,341
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter if it's in five minutes or 50 years.

545
00:41:06,922 --> 00:41:12,545
[SPEAKER_00]: You've... the life, the true meaning of life, would be that which is not

546
00:41:13,771 --> 00:41:14,772
[SPEAKER_00]: permeable by death.

547
00:41:15,092 --> 00:41:16,592
[SPEAKER_00]: That which is not subjected to death.

548
00:41:16,692 --> 00:41:17,673
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be the true life.

549
00:41:18,573 --> 00:41:20,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Or this is... this is beyond death.

550
00:41:21,914 --> 00:41:24,875
[SPEAKER_00]: But you realize only understanding of death can be beyond death.

551
00:41:26,016 --> 00:41:26,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything else is within it.

552
00:41:27,356 --> 00:41:31,498
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have no understanding of death, means your whole life is already as if dead.

553
00:41:42,462 --> 00:41:48,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Peripheral awareness of that context will take you out of the confinements of it, through understanding.

554
00:41:52,049 --> 00:41:53,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like you're going to develop something outside of it.

555
00:41:54,011 --> 00:41:54,691
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

556
00:41:55,252 --> 00:42:09,523
[SPEAKER_00]: You're going to just lose any passion, any appropriation, any relationship with it, with everything that's liable to death, which is five aggregates, which is your experience as a whole, your life, past, future, all of it.

557
00:42:12,955 --> 00:42:15,216
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about destroying it and not having it arise.

558
00:42:15,876 --> 00:42:22,658
[SPEAKER_00]: No, because that's... that's something you're experiencing, which means it's another thing that has a reason for you.

559
00:42:23,218 --> 00:42:26,699
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about removing the emotional connection and appropriation of it.

560
00:42:31,560 --> 00:42:38,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will know you're removing it, whereby if you think about losing your life, is there concern arising, is there fear, is there worry?

561
00:42:38,942 --> 00:42:39,122
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

562
00:42:39,582 --> 00:42:39,942
[SPEAKER_00]: It's okay.

563
00:42:40,933 --> 00:42:43,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, I'm not holding to anything, even if death comes next second.

564
00:43:02,187 --> 00:43:07,150
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're practicing meditation on this, you allow it to endure for half an hour, forty minutes, an hour.

565
00:43:08,271 --> 00:43:09,892
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when you're not meditating,

566
00:43:10,248 --> 00:43:11,909
[SPEAKER_00]: You still allow it to endure when it's there.

567
00:43:12,650 --> 00:43:17,693
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it's not there as much, you try and make it back, come... bring it back, even while you have to do other things.

568
00:43:18,734 --> 00:43:23,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Because nothing in the world is an excuse for ignoring the fact that death is certain.

569
00:43:25,098 --> 00:43:26,239
[SPEAKER_00]: There's absolutely no excuse for that.

570
00:43:30,522 --> 00:43:35,846
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever you engage with, you make sure you keep the... keep... keep a corner of the eye on that fundamental context.

571
00:43:36,928 --> 00:43:43,130
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you are emulating and developing in a more controlled environment, which is what meditation is.

572
00:43:43,150 --> 00:43:55,874
[SPEAKER_00]: But not controlled environment of mechanical method, but controlled environment of reflection and discerning the signs and tokens of how... what are the implications... how does it feel, pretty much, of being liable to death at any given moment.

573
00:43:55,914 --> 00:43:57,934
[SPEAKER_00]: And you want to see it on that peripheral level.

574
00:43:58,275 --> 00:43:59,795
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, okay, I'm aware of it.

575
00:44:00,623 --> 00:44:03,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, the mind starts thinking about things you have to do, about things that happened yesterday.

576
00:44:04,244 --> 00:44:07,045
[SPEAKER_00]: But if that context is there, there will be no passion in regard to it.

577
00:44:07,606 --> 00:44:11,527
[SPEAKER_00]: You start losing the context, you re-establish it.

578
00:44:11,547 --> 00:44:12,948
[SPEAKER_01]: There's also an idea that when...

579
00:44:13,708 --> 00:44:19,910
[SPEAKER_01]: I must practice, so that when death happens, I'll be able to control it in a nice way.

580
00:44:19,990 --> 00:44:21,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because that's what I was describing before.

581
00:44:22,011 --> 00:44:25,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for people, they're so used to just focusing what's in front of them.

582
00:44:26,493 --> 00:44:29,094
[SPEAKER_00]: And they think, if I just learn the art of focusing,

583
00:44:29,918 --> 00:44:37,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of what nature of things that I'm focusing on is, then when death comes in front of me, I'll be able to, you know, attend it rightly.

584
00:44:38,201 --> 00:44:41,482
[SPEAKER_00]: But as we described in previous talks, attention is not what's in front of you.

585
00:44:42,242 --> 00:44:44,242
[SPEAKER_00]: The roots of your attention are from that peripheral.

586
00:44:44,963 --> 00:44:50,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means if you haven't addressed things on that peripheral level, by the time they're in front of you, it's too late.

587
00:44:50,524 --> 00:44:52,145
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can see that while you're still alive.

588
00:44:53,031 --> 00:45:08,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Because truly, I mean, if you developed your focusing so far, if that were to work, focusing on the one moment in front of you, after one session, that focusing, you would be completely free from desire, from ill-will, from fear, from anything unwholesome.

589
00:45:09,222 --> 00:45:13,925
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that you're not, means no amount of focusing can help you with that.

590
00:45:14,745 --> 00:45:18,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Only understanding that would then prevent it from affecting you in the first place.

591
00:45:22,087 --> 00:45:26,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that whole idea of I'll practice focusing so when the death comes I'll know how to deal with it.

592
00:45:27,270 --> 00:45:29,712
[SPEAKER_00]: That whole idea is rooted still in that view of management.

593
00:45:30,732 --> 00:45:34,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Management as in requiring to be affected and then learning how to manage it.

594
00:45:35,455 --> 00:45:38,437
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you want to uproot it whereby it doesn't affect you in the first place.

595
00:45:39,658 --> 00:45:47,783
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only way to reach the deathless in this life is to really abandon everything that is subjected to that death.

596
00:45:49,584 --> 00:45:51,305
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you will know that while you're still alive.

597
00:45:52,212 --> 00:45:56,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you wait for death to confirm it to you, means you don't know it.

598
00:45:57,494 --> 00:45:59,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Means there won't be a confirmation you want to hear.

599
00:46:00,775 --> 00:46:05,837
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Arahant already knows that death that has been abandoned and so on.

600
00:46:07,358 --> 00:46:16,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Even before he dies.

601
00:46:16,502 --> 00:46:19,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Before preparation, before... prepare yourself.

602
00:46:20,987 --> 00:46:38,991
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to develop, if you want to practice towards not being affected by the experience of death when it comes, you start practicing towards not being affected by any experience in this life, even now.

603
00:46:39,011 --> 00:46:45,932
[SPEAKER_00]: So, am I affected by the arisen incitement of centrality, or ill-will, or distraction, or carelessness?

604
00:46:46,432 --> 00:46:48,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I'm affected by it.

605
00:46:48,593 --> 00:46:49,253
[SPEAKER_00]: That's my work.

606
00:46:49,353 --> 00:46:50,373
[SPEAKER_00]: I shouldn't be affected by it.

607
00:46:50,849 --> 00:46:56,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I'm not affected by that, I won't be affected by anything that comes through that... that same means of arising.

608
00:46:58,471 --> 00:46:59,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's death itself.

609
00:47:07,273 --> 00:47:13,955
[SPEAKER_00]: But people think, I'll just do this focusing practice and that's somehow magically gonna result in me... controlling the death experience.

610
00:47:13,995 --> 00:47:14,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

611
00:47:14,495 --> 00:47:17,836
[SPEAKER_00]: When death comes, I will, you know, I'll be in charge.

612
00:47:17,876 --> 00:47:18,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, are you in charge now?

613
00:47:19,442 --> 00:47:22,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you say no to yourself when sensuality presents itself?

614
00:47:24,803 --> 00:47:26,784
[SPEAKER_00]: How many people can answer yes to that question?

615
00:47:28,244 --> 00:47:32,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I'm not even affected in the first place, let alone I have to manage my restraint.

616
00:47:32,546 --> 00:47:34,886
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I'm not affected in the first place.

617
00:47:34,966 --> 00:47:41,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, here you are expecting to magically be able to deal with death, simply on account of some mechanical nostril watching practice.

618
00:47:46,084 --> 00:47:52,028
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way you won't be affected by death is if the life itself stops affecting you.

619
00:47:56,511 --> 00:47:58,132
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you practice not being affected by life?

620
00:47:58,632 --> 00:48:06,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can only do so while you're still alive.

621
00:48:07,918 --> 00:48:14,442
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only do so by discerning what being affected is, by admitting it to yourself, by uncovering it, by understanding it.

622
00:48:17,973 --> 00:48:23,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you know what it is, you have a sense as well as to what you have to do in order to not be affected by it.

623
00:48:32,764 --> 00:48:33,424
[SPEAKER_00]: What life is.

624
00:48:34,085 --> 00:48:34,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

625
00:48:35,346 --> 00:48:36,887
[SPEAKER_00]: The experiences you have in your life.

626
00:48:46,802 --> 00:48:50,104
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always a context, yeah.

627
00:48:50,524 --> 00:48:55,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Always, internally, there's always a context, which is why people spend most of the time covering it up.

628
00:48:56,968 --> 00:49:00,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it always reveals you that which you don't want to see necessarily.

629
00:49:01,031 --> 00:49:07,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Or rather, it takes time and effort to be able to get used to seeing it and then not be suffering on account of it.

630
00:49:09,015 --> 00:49:13,218
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the life, saṃsara and the whole sensuality goes with the grain.

631
00:49:14,721 --> 00:49:16,723
[SPEAKER_00]: It's effortless to turn a blind eye.

632
00:49:17,403 --> 00:49:19,125
[SPEAKER_00]: It's effortless to engage with centrality.

633
00:49:19,725 --> 00:49:31,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to start making the effort to pull yourself out of it before you'll be able to appreciate then the restraint, solitude, non-distraction, non-ill-will.

634
00:49:31,816 --> 00:49:35,720
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't naturally value those things.

635
00:49:37,621 --> 00:49:38,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Effort and wisdom.

636
00:49:43,773 --> 00:49:54,002
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the meditation practice, allowing that context, concerning context of death to endure, means allowing a degree of fear to arise.

637
00:49:55,744 --> 00:50:01,109
[SPEAKER_00]: And not, don't, don't give in to trying to get rid of it, right away.

638
00:50:02,190 --> 00:50:05,813
[SPEAKER_00]: But allow it to be there, while then the mind is thinking other things.

639
00:50:06,854 --> 00:50:07,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't automatically

640
00:50:08,322 --> 00:50:11,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Fear, unpleasant, I must get rid of this.

641
00:50:11,625 --> 00:50:15,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever I do, I must get rid of this anxiety on account of context of death.

642
00:50:18,170 --> 00:50:18,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

643
00:50:18,951 --> 00:50:19,872
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to get rid of it.

644
00:50:20,913 --> 00:50:22,314
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't affect you do anything.

645
00:50:22,334 --> 00:50:24,516
[SPEAKER_00]: You're the one who chooses to do things on account of it.

646
00:50:25,196 --> 00:50:27,278
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize I can choose not to do anything on account of it.

647
00:50:27,818 --> 00:50:30,961
[SPEAKER_00]: I can just allow it to be there for as long as he wants to be.

648
00:50:31,982 --> 00:50:35,665
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you automatically strengthen your mind.

649
00:50:36,423 --> 00:50:40,005
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you automatically uncover anything you've been covering up.

650
00:50:40,045 --> 00:50:41,106
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, it's gonna take time.

651
00:50:42,706 --> 00:50:44,627
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you invest a lot of time in covering it up.

652
00:50:46,609 --> 00:50:49,110
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the context of death is enduring.

653
00:50:49,130 --> 00:50:53,752
[SPEAKER_00]: You're seated, in your room, in your kuti, whatever, a layperson, a monk, not distracted.

654
00:50:55,753 --> 00:50:56,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

655
00:50:56,354 --> 00:50:57,474
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't even have to watch it.

656
00:50:57,514 --> 00:50:57,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Watch it.

657
00:50:59,616 --> 00:51:02,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Just don't rush into overly defining it necessarily.

658
00:51:02,617 --> 00:51:05,899
[SPEAKER_00]: That's gonna become rooted in trying to get rid of that unpleasant aspect of it.

659
00:51:06,815 --> 00:51:07,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Just allow it to endure.

660
00:51:08,897 --> 00:51:13,921
[SPEAKER_00]: And sometimes that endurance will be clearer, as in it's going to be more present, sometimes it will be less.

661
00:51:14,382 --> 00:51:16,904
[SPEAKER_00]: But you still recognize it to a degree it's still there.

662
00:51:18,225 --> 00:51:28,294
[SPEAKER_00]: So then you... if there is nothing else going on, because it shouldn't be, you're in that protected meditation environment, no distractions, as I said, let it endure.

663
00:51:29,295 --> 00:51:30,236
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you have a thought of

664
00:51:30,973 --> 00:51:32,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, after this, I must do this, this, this.

665
00:51:33,075 --> 00:51:38,840
[SPEAKER_00]: And that, now, you look at, you see that thought against that backdrop of the context of death.

666
00:51:39,701 --> 00:51:45,205
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, things that you must do, your duties, are now seen against the context of death.

667
00:51:46,306 --> 00:51:49,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you have a memory of something that happened in the past that bothers you.

668
00:51:50,610 --> 00:51:53,893
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, against the context of death, that memory has arisen.

669
00:51:54,653 --> 00:51:56,995
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already, you're not as bothered as much.

670
00:51:57,616 --> 00:51:59,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you got a bigger picture now.

671
00:52:00,464 --> 00:52:04,606
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not sucked into what bothers you, because you stopped trying to get rid of the unpleasant.

672
00:52:05,127 --> 00:52:07,948
[SPEAKER_00]: And now you have the ultimate unpleasant, the prospect of death.

673
00:52:09,809 --> 00:52:12,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Any other more inferior unpleasant won't be unpleasant.

674
00:52:14,972 --> 00:52:18,034
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if those thoughts present themselves, you let them present themselves.

675
00:52:18,514 --> 00:52:19,575
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't try to get rid of it.

676
00:52:19,635 --> 00:52:21,376
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't try to overanalyze it, overthink it.

677
00:52:21,396 --> 00:52:28,840
[SPEAKER_00]: You just make sure, don't forget about a context of that certain death that I'm concerned about right now in my meditation.

678
00:52:30,180 --> 00:52:32,282
[SPEAKER_00]: While I let my mind think what it wants to think.

679
00:52:34,784 --> 00:52:41,929
[SPEAKER_00]: And the point will come, if you keep doing it, whereby the context will be immovable, imperturbable, in regard to whatever your mind thinks.

680
00:52:42,410 --> 00:52:55,300
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will recognize also that the only way to perturb it, is to intentionally choose to do so, by saying yes to sensuality carelessly, saying yes to ill-will carelessly, saying yes to distraction carelessly.

681
00:53:00,424 --> 00:53:01,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'll think about that later.

682
00:53:02,245 --> 00:53:04,006
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already a careless choice.

683
00:53:05,867 --> 00:53:07,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not thinking about that context.

684
00:53:07,708 --> 00:53:08,688
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point I'm making.

685
00:53:09,229 --> 00:53:12,350
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not, I'm thinking about it, I'm dead, I'm gonna be dead, I'm gonna be dead.

686
00:53:12,370 --> 00:53:12,550
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

687
00:53:12,911 --> 00:53:14,251
[SPEAKER_00]: It's there, enduring on its own.

688
00:53:14,551 --> 00:53:18,574
[SPEAKER_00]: You're actually thinking about your duties, or things that bother you, or things that you like.

689
00:53:19,394 --> 00:53:20,254
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you're thinking.

690
00:53:20,875 --> 00:53:23,476
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, on the basis of the context that you discerned.

691
00:53:25,497 --> 00:53:29,019
[SPEAKER_00]: So that context is there, peripherally, as a container of all these things that you do.

692
00:53:30,030 --> 00:53:32,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means there will be less and less ground.

693
00:53:32,351 --> 00:53:37,554
[SPEAKER_00]: The longer that context endures, less and less basis for passion towards these particular things will be.

694
00:53:38,454 --> 00:53:41,536
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is effortless as well, because you're not thinking about it.

695
00:53:42,316 --> 00:53:45,357
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need to do is abstain from intentionally covering it up.

696
00:53:47,118 --> 00:53:48,339
[SPEAKER_00]: And abstaining is effortless.

697
00:53:48,639 --> 00:53:50,040
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't require you to do something.

698
00:53:50,520 --> 00:53:52,281
[SPEAKER_00]: It just requires you to not do something.

699
00:53:55,723 --> 00:53:56,843
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's what meditation is.

700
00:53:57,523 --> 00:53:58,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever your object.

701
00:53:59,264 --> 00:54:08,787
[SPEAKER_00]: That's to say, it establishes his mind in the divine abiding of the jhāna, and whether he sits, walks, urinates, defecates, eats, his mind is divine in that state.

702
00:54:28,010 --> 00:54:30,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Because all these things are inferior to his establishment.

703
00:54:31,352 --> 00:54:34,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Establish your mind in the context of death, certain death.

704
00:54:35,974 --> 00:54:46,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely no... not even a grain of opportunity for passion to arise, if that context fully saturates your mind.

705
00:54:47,899 --> 00:54:48,679
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's effortless.

706
00:54:49,680 --> 00:54:51,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the context is on the... there, on its own.

707
00:54:52,761 --> 00:54:54,442
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what things are generally subjected to.

708
00:54:54,462 --> 00:54:54,982
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a fact.

709
00:54:55,042 --> 00:54:56,243
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a factual context.

710
00:54:57,561 --> 00:55:01,923
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason it's not saturating anything is because you're covering it up.

711
00:55:01,983 --> 00:55:02,683
[SPEAKER_00]: You covered it up.

712
00:55:03,964 --> 00:55:09,906
[SPEAKER_00]: And you keep maintaining that covering up through engaging with unwholesome centrality of will and so on.

713
00:55:11,507 --> 00:55:12,567
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't create this?

714
00:55:13,528 --> 00:55:13,908
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

715
00:55:13,948 --> 00:55:14,968
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

716
00:55:15,269 --> 00:55:16,809
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, you don't create the dhamma.

717
00:55:17,810 --> 00:55:20,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the Buddha said it's just an ancient path that was covered.

718
00:55:20,331 --> 00:55:21,011
[SPEAKER_00]: He uncovered it.

719
00:55:21,091 --> 00:55:22,011
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't create anything.

720
00:55:22,832 --> 00:55:25,653
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just discernment of these factual universal truths.

721
00:55:29,337 --> 00:55:32,278
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what meditation should be revolving around.

722
00:55:33,118 --> 00:55:36,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter if you do it for 15 minutes or for 5 hours.

723
00:55:37,819 --> 00:55:46,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Establish your mind upon the greater context that... that is infertile basis for any passion, any ill-will.

724
00:55:47,401 --> 00:55:49,402
[SPEAKER_00]: They cannot exist on that context.

725
00:55:50,022 --> 00:55:52,843
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you let the context endure until your mind gets established in it.

726
00:55:54,823 --> 00:55:58,464
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know your mind is established in it when you cannot be passionate.

727
00:55:59,914 --> 00:56:01,735
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot be affected by the arisen feeling.

728
00:56:03,437 --> 00:56:04,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant, unpleasant or neutral.

729
00:56:06,880 --> 00:56:15,046
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you also know when you're about to do something, if it's gonna make you firmly, more firmly established in that context, or it's gonna take you away from it.

730
00:56:16,087 --> 00:56:17,248
[SPEAKER_00]: The choice becomes much clearer.

731
00:56:18,068 --> 00:56:19,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is unwholesome, this is wholesome.

732
00:56:20,690 --> 00:56:23,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Unwholesome means takes you away from the wholesome context.

733
00:56:24,053 --> 00:56:26,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Wholesome means keeps you within a wholesome context.

734
00:56:29,336 --> 00:56:34,418
[SPEAKER_00]: On the basis within that domain, doesn't contradict it, doesn't abolish it, doesn't drag you down from it.

735
00:56:46,183 --> 00:56:52,926
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why like Devadatta in the suttas, once he started engaging in unwholesome acts, he was unable to establish his mind in jhānas.

736
00:56:57,110 --> 00:57:01,692
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't some magical energy that got trapped or evaporated.

737
00:57:01,852 --> 00:57:01,992
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

738
00:57:02,612 --> 00:57:05,293
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just unwholesome, contradicts the domain of it.

739
00:57:05,813 --> 00:57:10,795
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you choose to engage in it, you choose to then forsake this, the greater context.

740
00:57:12,916 --> 00:57:23,180
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only way to develop greater context is to keep discerning it, keep thinking about it, keep trying to establish it, allowing it to endure, not trying to get rid of it if it's unpleasant.

741
00:57:38,363 --> 00:57:40,084
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you can't crave for it either.

742
00:57:40,284 --> 00:57:41,464
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't crave for jhānas.

743
00:57:41,964 --> 00:57:44,465
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't crave for the imperturbable, wholesome samādhi.

744
00:57:44,886 --> 00:57:47,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Because craving is always towards the actual.

745
00:57:49,548 --> 00:57:51,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling in regard to the actual thing.

746
00:57:51,428 --> 00:57:52,309
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you crave for.

747
00:57:53,349 --> 00:57:54,570
[SPEAKER_00]: But this is the context of it.

748
00:57:55,390 --> 00:58:02,293
[SPEAKER_00]: And now your mind is establishing that, which means your mind then became unreachable by any craving.

749
00:58:03,573 --> 00:58:05,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, no room for anything that's based on that craving.

750
00:58:08,282 --> 00:58:11,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality, ill-will, delusion and distraction.

751
00:58:16,407 --> 00:58:19,229
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if people wonder, how do I make this context endure?

752
00:58:19,810 --> 00:58:20,510
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't do it.

753
00:58:22,151 --> 00:58:24,433
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't, oh, it's enduring, I'm making it endure.

754
00:58:24,833 --> 00:58:27,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's like you still trying to make it actual in front of you.

755
00:58:31,177 --> 00:58:34,660
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you make it endure, but stop trying to make it endure.

756
00:58:36,401 --> 00:58:38,122
[SPEAKER_00]: You make it endure by thinking about it,

757
00:58:40,058 --> 00:58:41,719
[SPEAKER_00]: But by not interfering about it either.

758
00:58:45,361 --> 00:58:55,025
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the same sense, how do you... like, as we gave the example of the appointment, life-changing appointment you have, you don't need to make it endure.

759
00:58:55,205 --> 00:58:56,026
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already there.

760
00:58:56,726 --> 00:58:58,967
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need to do is stop distracting yourself from it.

761
00:59:00,548 --> 00:59:05,731
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't cancel that appointment.

762
00:59:06,511 --> 00:59:06,651
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

763
00:59:07,051 --> 00:59:08,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can't cancel this appointment.

764
00:59:10,250 --> 00:59:15,774
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, okay, the recognition of that, it's already a form of endurance, even if I don't necessarily feel fully concerned about it.

765
00:59:17,895 --> 00:59:23,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Still, I am aware the context of whatever I do, it's gonna end.

766
00:59:24,560 --> 00:59:25,581
[SPEAKER_01]: All of it is subjected.

767
00:59:25,681 --> 00:59:28,362
[SPEAKER_01]: It can end even before I... before I realize it will end.

768
00:59:30,404 --> 00:59:32,005
[SPEAKER_00]: My beating heart right now can stop.

769
00:59:33,066 --> 00:59:34,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Have that... allow that thought to endure.

770
00:59:39,593 --> 00:59:41,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't overthink it.

771
00:59:41,114 --> 00:59:42,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't give in to fear of it.

772
00:59:42,135 --> 00:59:44,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Just allow it to endure.

773
00:59:44,658 --> 00:59:45,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Allow that thought to endure.

774
00:59:46,559 --> 00:59:50,082
[SPEAKER_00]: And then see other things that appear in your mind against enduring of that thought.

775
00:59:50,363 --> 00:59:52,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not looking for a specific answer.

776
00:59:52,545 --> 00:59:54,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is what it is, or this is how it will be.

777
00:59:55,307 --> 01:00:04,716
[SPEAKER_00]: You're looking for the, like, evoking of the context of being liable to death.

778
01:00:04,776 --> 01:00:06,177
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you create that connection.

779
01:00:08,483 --> 01:00:16,625
[SPEAKER_00]: So, while I'm sitting here, breathing, secluded from unwholesome, secluded from distraction, what if this beating muscle in my chest were to stop?

780
01:00:17,865 --> 01:00:18,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't answer it.

781
01:00:20,465 --> 01:00:22,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't provide an excuse or an explanation.

782
01:00:22,926 --> 01:00:25,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Just allow that question to sink in.

783
01:00:26,726 --> 01:00:27,787
[SPEAKER_00]: What if it were to stop?

784
01:00:29,327 --> 01:00:32,148
[SPEAKER_00]: And then mind, well, this, this, this, all that is secondary.

785
01:00:32,168 --> 01:00:34,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Still, allow just the question to endure.

786
01:00:43,110 --> 01:00:44,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the thought comes, it will stop.

787
01:00:44,891 --> 01:00:45,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Allow that to endure.

788
01:00:47,773 --> 01:00:50,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, your timer rings, your meditation finished.

789
01:00:51,575 --> 01:00:52,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, fine.

790
01:00:52,416 --> 01:00:56,719
[SPEAKER_00]: But you realize that context still hasn't changed.

791
01:00:56,759 --> 01:00:58,220
[SPEAKER_00]: So, okay, you go and do your duty now.

792
01:00:58,600 --> 01:01:00,121
[SPEAKER_00]: And you still have that context enduring.

793
01:01:03,483 --> 01:01:10,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will forget it only when you engage in unwholesome, lust, aversion or delusion.

794
01:01:12,425 --> 01:01:19,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, yes, you need to meditate in this manner as much as you can, until that context becomes imperturbable.

795
01:01:22,232 --> 01:01:39,163
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could also see you need, like, a basis of sense restraint, physical restraint, as a... as a... as a guardian, whereby your context is lost, but your bodily behavior, speech behavior, even your mental behavior, doesn't take you as far back into the unwholesome.

796
01:01:39,523 --> 01:01:40,824
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you won't have to undo it as much.

797
01:01:42,371 --> 01:01:43,612
[SPEAKER_00]: And the virtue comes first.

798
01:01:43,692 --> 01:01:44,292
[SPEAKER_00]: It's necessary.

799
01:01:51,796 --> 01:01:59,780
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the context is enduring now, you still haven't forgotten that your beating heart might stop, that you're fundamentally liable to death, you have a degree of concern.

800
01:02:01,261 --> 01:02:01,921
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I do now?

801
01:02:03,262 --> 01:02:06,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you include the thought of what do I do now against that context.

802
01:02:07,244 --> 01:02:08,705
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize you don't do anything.

803
01:02:12,261 --> 01:02:13,182
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to do anything.

804
01:02:17,324 --> 01:02:18,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the context still there?

805
01:02:18,645 --> 01:02:18,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

806
01:02:23,388 --> 01:02:27,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever were to happen to me, would it be against the backdrop of that context?

807
01:02:27,671 --> 01:02:27,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

808
01:02:28,171 --> 01:02:34,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever I were to do with my life, choose immediately or for the future, is this context changeable?

809
01:02:34,195 --> 01:02:34,335
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

810
01:02:38,498 --> 01:02:41,760
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are my engagements with life or with people, are they worthy?

811
01:02:43,823 --> 01:02:54,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Against this context, in this new light of the new context, is it worthy being like, you know, doing this or doing that, or being so emotionally disturbed and kind of not getting what I want?

812
01:02:56,553 --> 01:02:57,514
[SPEAKER_00]: No, really.

813
01:02:57,534 --> 01:02:58,314
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't even then...

814
01:02:58,735 --> 01:03:08,863
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're in that context, being affected by things such as sensuality and so on, if you're truly in that context, being affected by those things becomes inconceivable.

815
01:03:09,383 --> 01:03:10,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even at that very time.

816
01:03:11,053 --> 01:03:13,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure, you can forget about the context and then be affected by it.

817
01:03:14,175 --> 01:03:16,517
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you're in the context, it is truly imperturbable.

818
01:03:17,317 --> 01:03:23,782
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all you need to do is find ways of maintaining the context, regardless whether you're sitting in a protected meditation environment or not.

819
01:03:25,543 --> 01:03:30,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, seated, walking, lying down, extending his arms, eating, urinating, defecating, whatever you do.

820
01:03:31,688 --> 01:03:32,708
[SPEAKER_00]: You maintain the context.

821
01:03:34,350 --> 01:03:34,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Day and night.

822
01:03:37,972 --> 01:03:43,279
[SPEAKER_00]: To the extent necessary for obliterating all, any ground for any passion to manifest again.

823
01:03:43,719 --> 01:03:49,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Passion, not things that you're passionate towards.

824
01:03:51,409 --> 01:03:55,034
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, that's the meditation object of death.

